  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/a2d02d14edf25a59eceb842a2cc902f07a29754f0978ec56a8293e16698ec383.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}={160}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}+{20}^{∘}={200}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}={340}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta $ cannot be equal to ${260}^{∘}.$",
